  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 251 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nA cold front will trail along a negatively tilted upper wave near \nthe US-Canada border and has trended slower to exit over Iowa. Heavy \nrain is the main concern as PWATs rise above 1.5\" and flow points \nparallel to the front's orientation. While CAPE totals over 1000 \nJ/kg both Tuesday and Wednesday afternoon, the saturation of the \nprofile keeps lapse rates closer to the moist adiabat, presenting an \nelongated and skinny CAPE profile. Uniform parcel acceleration will \nfavor heavy rain over hail and the weak shear profile will make any \nwet microburst potential in storms relatively short-lived. The \nsevere weather risk is low for these reasons on both days, but a \nMarginal and Slight Risk ERO have been issued. Rainfall will peak in \nthe evening and overnight hours with deterministic runs all painting \na band of 2\"+ in northern and central Iowa. Given present convective \nprocesses highlighted above, would not be surprised if localized \namounts over 5\" are realized. Amounts will be better understood as \nthe system enters the CAM window.\n\nFor hydrology impacts, soil moisture in northern and northwest Iowa \nis low (5-20%) and can take the most rain. National Water Model \nsimulations using GFS QPF have most of the river responses in \neastern Iowa where antecedent conditions are comparatively more\nwet. Only the high-end basin QPF outputs bring basins such as \nthe Cedar into Flood Stage, so the chance for this is low (5%).\nFor flash flood potential, Iowa is in peak crop maturity which \nfurther diminishes concerns for rural parts of the state. \nConcerns increase if the axis of heavy rain falls on an urban \narea.",
